at's more than double the number of  last year  the paperwork used to take about 20 days  to process  now it takes months  that's left thousands of migrants from  haiti and central america  stranded in tapachula struggling to  survive  several ngos say it's part of a  deliberate political strategy  to dissuade the migrants from leaving  their countries the un's refugee agency  says governments need to do more  we are calling for a greater effort from  all countries  it's not just one country that has to  deal with this situation  we're seeing it across the region and  all the leaders have to be involved  dania and her family spent all their  savings to flee the violence in their  neighborhood  anthony's father was recently killed by  gang members  the people who killed the boy's father  are threatening us too  we don't want to go back to honduras no  locked in this van and worried about  deportation  they fear they've invested everything  just to end up  back where they started  well for more let's speak to liz shulker  she's a u.s policy analyst at the  humboldt foundation here in berlin  so liz this is harris's first foreign  trip as vice president  what do you make of it so far  well this is a huge moment for her um  she  is a coming from the the congress the  senate background and this is going to  test her foreign policy chops  and she needs to do it very wisely so  kind of what i'm hearing  coming from the administration is to set  modest expectations  but a strong foundation so they're not  looking for her to eradicate  migrant policy or our uh relationship  with immigration  but to set things in place that moving  forward  signal the biden administration is  taking this very seriously  and uh vice president harris the right  person to lead that  now kamala harris is telling would-be  migrants rather bluntly  do not come that's quite a turnaround  from the welcoming tone we heard from  the biden administration initially  isn't it i'm not sure i would phrase i